  "query_preview": "../Tuesday through Thursday night/\n\nAn active Fall pattern continues through the extended forecast, with \nshort breaks of dry conditions in between passing systems.\nMaritime interests should remain aware of Gale force fronts \npushing across the Gulf and along the coast on Tuesday and \nThursday.\n\nA long wave upper level trough over the Aleutian Chain swings the \nfirst of many fronts across the Gulf through Tuesday, with \nwidespread sustained Gale force winds 35 to 45 kts and storm force \ngusts up to 55 kts across the Gulf and along the coast from Cape \nDecision northward to Cape Suckling through the day.  Overland, \nstrong winds remain possible across the central and southern \npanhandle given current forecast orientation of the front with gusts \nu for Prince of Wales, Ketchikan, and Metlakata by late Tueday night \ninto Wednesday afternoon. By Wednesday night, a seperate, but \nstronger low develops in the northern Pacific as it deppens to \n~970mb by Thursday morning, and moves into the southern Gulf of \nAlaska. This low will swing a strong front acros the gulf through \nThursday, bringing another round of sustained Gale force winds 35 to \n45 kts and storm force gusts up to 55 kts across the Gulf and along \nthe coast from Dixon Entrance northward to the Fairweather Grounds \nthrough the day.  Strongest overlands winds once again focused \nacross the sotuerh npanhandle, however albiet less forecast \nconfdience on location and expected gusts, but would not be suprised \nto see gusts up to 35 mph by Thursday evening for Kethickan and \nMetlakata (looking at you Yellow Hill).\n\nAs of now, no significant QPF concerns with this system given how \nfast each front is expected to push through. Periods of moderate to \nheavy rain are expected, but not expecting a long duration rainfall \nthreat. Current rainfall amounts with each front is around 0.5-1.5\" \nacross the northern and central panhandle, 1-2\" for the southern \npanhandle.",
